A||||8||loafer87gt||Need some longblock info ||||1||
Z||000000||loafer87gt||01-19-2000||09:04 PM||||My 2.8 in my 87 GT is in rough shape. Like I mentioned earlier, the bearings and rods are shot, and the timing chain and such also needs to be replaced. I was going to rebuild the engine using hi-po parts until I got some quotes for the work. The cheapest quote was around 2900 bucks. I was thinking I might be better off buying a longblock engine instead, having the added safety of a warranty. I was checking out some different sources of longblocks, and found this page.<BR> <A HREF="http://automotiveengine.com/lb_gm_6cl.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://automotiveengine.com/lb_gm_6cl.htm</A> <P>Can someone tell me which engines on this page will be a direct replacement for the 2.8. Is the V6 in the GT's a FWD or RWD engine? Also, could a person use a newer 3.1 with the aluminum heads.Any info would be much appreciated. Thanks<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Phaeton||01-19-2000||10:23 PM||||Aluminum head 3.1 engines work fine with the correct ECM setup.  Should be a thread on the physical work to fit it in the Fiero.  The Chevy dealer has an aluminum block to go with the heads, and it is already set up for installation in a Fiero. $3000US for the bare block, ouch.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DRH||01-20-2000||02:11 PM||||Another option is a NEW GM 3.4 long block. Seems like I heard these are less than $2000 (someone correct me if I'm all wet on price) You can use the Fiero intake, electronics, etc. It does require some modification to work (the main one being drilling new starter mounting holes).<P>This link gives some info on what it takes: <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#34" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/fierofactory/start.html#34</A> <P>This is a link to GMs site: <A HREF="http://www.gmgoodwrench.com/crate_engine/global/ix_ht34.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.gmgoodwrench.com/crate_engine/global/ix_ht34.htm</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||cobra427||01-20-2000||04:09 PM||||For all the 3.4 buffs,Dobles GM Parts Depot in N.H. 1-800-842-9600, is selling until 3/1/00, the 160 hp 3.4 engine #12363230 for $1725.00. Just thought you'd like to know.||||reg||1
Z||000004||loafer87gt||01-20-2000||06:33 PM||||Hi guys,<P>I talked to my local GM dealer and he can order me the GM performance Plus 3.4 Crate engine for $1693. The heads have large 1.72" intake and 1.42" exhaust valves and combustion chambers that yield a healthy 9 to 1 compression ratio. The camshaft is a high lift, short duration design, developed to produce lots of strong low end torque and mid range horsepower. With .427" intake and .454" exhaust lift, the cam ensures a strong pull to 5000 RPM. (Ripped from their site) The only problem with this is that they offer no warranty on the engine if I drill the new starter hole. The price is excellent though, only $200 more than a stock 2.8 longblock. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||cobra427||01-20-2000||08:49 PM||||loafer, the 3.4 uses essentially the same head as the Fiero 2.8 uses as far as the valves go.Both have the 1.72x1.42 configurement. Not sure what the cc volume is in the heads tho but I'll bet they are close.||||reg||1
Z||000006||sluppy123||01-20-2000||09:44 PM||||I paid a little over $800 for my Jasper longblock, with that I got a high vol. Sealed power oil pump and a 3yr. warranty. <P>Shop around and you can get a decent price on any longblock.<P>-Brian<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||WKDFIRO||01-21-2000||12:34 AM||||The heads are identical. As a matter of fact the 2.8s used in the Camaros and Firebirds had smaller valves. The Fiero apparently got what would best described as the 2.8 H.O.<P>Woohoo for us Fiero owners!||||reg||1
Z||000008||DRH||01-21-2000||01:02 PM||||loafer87gt, On the warranty and drilled holes... might be a little late... but if you don't tell them I doubt they would ever notice.||||reg||1
